provenance.prompt:
J.111 / Piper Quickstep — STEAL action sheet, attempt 1
Generate one clean game-art source sheet, exactly 1536 × 1024 pixels, transparent background, arranged as a precise 4-column × 2-row grid of eight equal 384 × 512 px cells. Every cell contains exactly ONE complete pose of the SAME Piper Quickstep. Read order left-to-right across row 1, then row 2. Keep each full figure, including every ponytail strand, fingertip, and shoe, wholly inside that cell, with at least 60 px transparent clearance from every cell edge. Figure silhouettes should be about 240 px wide and 360 px tall, consistently scaled and aligned. Preserve clear empty gutters between cells. No overlap, cropping, touch, crossing, or bleed at any cell boundary. True transparent alpha only outside the characters.

Use the supplied Piper images strictly as identity, anatomy, uniform, and rendering references; do not copy their poses. Piper is a fully human adult athletic woman with warm brown skin, strong but natural basketball-player proportions, expressive brown eyes, bright orange hair in a high swinging ponytail tied behind her head, russet-red sleeveless basketball jersey and shorts with cream/white piping and paired side stripes, red-and-cream high-top basketball shoes, white socks. Keep her same face, skin tone, hair color/style, uniform palette and polished hand-painted comic-game shading in all eight poses. Uniform remains completely blank: no words, numbers, logos, crests, jewelry, tattoos, or extra accessories. Correct human anatomy: exactly one head, two arms, two hands, five distinct natural fingers on each visible hand, two legs and two shoes; anatomically plausible shoulders, elbows, wrists, knees, and ankles. No ball in the artwork: the basketball is a separate moving game object.

Right-facing three-quarter profile throughout, readable dynamic basketball defense. Show eight DISTINCT consecutive steal action poses, complete full-body silhouettes:
1) low balanced ready defensive stance, knees bent, both empty hands open at chest/waist, eyes focused forward;
2) controlled weight shift toward the opponent, lead foot stepping forward, hips and shoulders beginning to turn, hands still open;
3) lower forward shuffle, torso leans forward, one hand begins a low swipe while the other guards space;
4) signature steal contact at tick 5: a decisive but balanced forward lunge, lead/right-facing arm fully extended toward image-right at about shoulder to head height with clearly spread open fingers, reaching hand well separated from the head, ponytail, face, and other hand; opposite arm visibly bent and counterbalancing behind/near torso; both legs distinct and a clear planted support leg. Make this silhouette strikingly different from every other cell. Keep the hand empty; do not draw any ball.
5) after the swipe, reaching arm begins to sweep inward across her front, shoulders recover and opposite hand remains visible;
6) gather elbows toward ribs while stepping through, still empty-handed; do not mime holding or catching an object;
7) take a short recovery step back and turn eyes toward the loose game ball offscreen, both hands open and empty;
8) settle into a poised low ready defensive stance distinct in limb spacing from pose 1.

Keep action readable as an athletic steal, not a block, shot, pass, catch, or celebration. No second character, no basketball, no props, no text, no panel outlines, no grid lines, no floor, no court, no shadow, no glow, no backdrop, no gradients, no lighting effects, no decorative marks. Strong pose separation, clear negative space around every limb, crisp clean alpha edges.
